---
layout: base.njk
title: Pricing
description: "$999 a month, or $9,990 a year with two months free. Unlimited runs, your whole team in, no seats to count, money back if it doesn't earn its keep."
# One page per language (#114) — see src/index.njk for the reasoning.
pagination:
  data: languages
  size: 1
  alias: language
  addAllPagesToCollections: true
permalink: "{{ language.prefix }}/pricing/"
---

{#- #153, 24 Aug 2026. /#pricing was a nav item pointing at an anchor on the
    homepage. This is the page it should have been.

    THE ONE RULE THIS PAGE MUST NOT BREAK (CLAUDE.md, and the reason it is
    written down there): the price CTA goes STRAIGHT TO STRIPE. It was once
    asked for exactly that, built as a link to an onboarding page, and recorded
    on the board as shipped for three days. So the markup here is the homepage
    tile's markup, ids and all, driven by the same buy.js — one field, then
    Stripe. The homepage tile stays where it is and keeps id="pricing", because
    Stripe's own cancel_url returns to /#pricing and deep links still land. -#}

{#- The one page on this site with a price on it, and until #137 the only
    machine-readable thing about it was the word "Pricing" in the <title>.
    `Product` + `Offer` is the type Google shows a price for, and the numbers
    here are not transcribed from the copy above: `99900` USD is what
    netlify/functions/create-checkout-session.js actually sends to Stripe
    (`unit_amount` and `currency`), which is the only figure that cannot drift
    away from what a buyer is charged. test/seo.test.js pins the two together,
    so changing the price in one place and not the other fails the suite.

    `brand` and `seller` point at the Organization node base.njk puts on every
    page. `priceValidUntil` is deliberately absent: it is optional for a
    subscription that has no end date, and an invented one expires.

    #542: two Offers, because there are now two ways to buy the same one
    workspace. `offers` takes a list for exactly this, and the test above reads
    BOTH against the PLANS table in the function — so a plan whose figure moves
    in one place and not the other fails the suite rather than reaching a
    search result. -#}
<script type="application/ld+json">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Product",
  "name": "Prospektor",
  "description": {{ ("A Prospektor workspace: unlimited pitch runs, research, decision-makers with sources, warm-intro mapping and every deliverable drafted in your voice." | t) | dump | safe }},
  "brand": { "@id": "{{ site.url }}/#organization" },
  "url": "{{ site.url }}{{ page.url }}",
  "image": "{{ site.url }}{{ '/assets/img/og.png' | asset }}",
  "offers": [
    {
      "@type": "Offer",
      "name": {{ ("Monthly" | t) | dump | safe }},
      "url": "{{ site.url }}{{ page.url }}",
      "price": "999.00",
      "priceCurrency": "USD",
      "availability": "https://schema.org/InStock",
      "seller": { "@id": "{{ site.url }}/#organization" },
      "priceSpecification": {
        "@type": "UnitPriceSpecification",
        "price": "999.00",
        "priceCurrency": "USD",
        "billingIncrement": 1,
        "unitText": "workspace",
        "billingDuration": 1,
        "billingPeriod": "P1M"
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Offer",
      "name": {{ ("Yearly" | t) | dump | safe }},
      "url": "{{ site.url }}{{ page.url }}",
      "price": "9990.00",
      "priceCurrency": "USD",
      "availability": "https://schema.org/InStock",
      "seller": { "@id": "{{ site.url }}/#organization" },
      "priceSpecification": {
        "@type": "UnitPriceSpecification",
        "price": "9990.00",
        "priceCurrency": "USD",
        "billingIncrement": 1,
        "unitText": "workspace",
        "billingDuration": 1,
        "billingPeriod": "P1Y"
      }
    }
  ]
}
</script>

<header class="page-hero">
  <div class="page-hero-inner">
    <span class="tag">{% t %}pricing{% endt %}</span>
    <h1 class="page-h1">{% t %}One price. The whole box.{% endt %}</h1>
    <p class="page-lede">{% t %}
      No seat maths, no credit meter, no sales call to find out the number.
      One workspace, one price, everything in it.
    {% endt %}</p>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pricing pricing-page">
  <div class="pricing-card">
    {#- #542 — the yearly plan. A SEGMENTED CONTROL, not a second buy button:
        a pricing page gets one primary action (the style skill, rule 8), and
        two CTAs is the shape the operator has twice asked us not to build.

        Both figures and both sentences are written here, by the build, in the
        reader's language; plan.js only decides which of them is hidden. So the
        switch can never say a price the build did not write, it costs a script
        no sentence of its own, and with JavaScript off the page is the monthly
        page it was — which is also what a crawler reads. -#}
    <div class="plan-switch" id="planSwitch" role="group" aria-label="{% t %}Billing period{% endt %}">
      <button type="button" class="plan-opt" data-plan="month" aria-pressed="true">{% t %}Monthly{% endt %}</button>
      <button type="button" class="plan-opt" data-plan="year" aria-pressed="false">{% t %}Yearly — 2 months free{% endt %}</button>
    </div>
    <div class="price-row" data-plan-show="month">
      <span class="price-num">$999</span>
      <span class="price-per">{% t %}/ month · per workspace{% endt %}</span>
    </div>
    <div class="price-row" data-plan-show="year" hidden>
      <span class="price-num">$9,990</span>
      <span class="price-per">{% t %}/ year · per workspace{% endt %}</span>
    </div>
    <p class="plan-note" data-plan-show="month">{% t %}Or <strong>$9,990 a year</strong> — two months free.{% endt %}</p>
    <p class="plan-note" data-plan-show="year" hidden>{% t %}<strong>Two months free</strong> on $11,988 — and the price is fixed for the year.{% endt %}</p>
    <p class="price-line">{% t %}Unlimited runs · your whole team in · money back if it doesn't earn its keep{% endt %}</p>

    <ul class="pricing-list">
      <li>{% t %}Unlimited pitch runs — research, scoring and every deliverable{% endt %}</li>
      <li>{% t %}Up to 100 companies in one batch, drafted while the tab is closed{% endt %}</li>
      <li>{% t %}Decision-makers with sources, and the confidence rating on each name{% endt %}</li>
      <li>{% t %}Your network mapped to warm paths, with the intro request drafted{% endt %}</li>
      <li>{% t %}Call prep, inbound evaluation and the outcome log that teaches the next run{% endt %}</li>
      <li>{% t %}Every colleague on your work-email domain, at no extra cost{% endt %}</li>
      <li>{% t %}Share links, print and PDF export of any deliverable{% endt %}</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="buy" id="buy">
      <a href="{{ '/checkout/' | localize }}" class="btn-cta" id="buyLink">{% t %}Start my studio →{% endt %}</a>
      <form class="reserve-form buy-form" id="buyForm" hidden novalidate>
        <input class="reserve-input" id="buyEmail" type="email" autocomplete="email"
               placeholder="you@yourcompany.com" aria-label="{% t %}Your work email{% endt %}">
        {#- The label carries the figure, so the yearly one is here too — read
            off the button by buy.js when the switch moves, which is also what
            it restores after a failed attempt. `| safe` because the entities
            are the sentence's own; without it Nunjucks would escape the `&`
            twice and the button would read "Pay &amp; start". -#}
        <button class="btn-cta" type="submit" id="buyBtn"
                data-label-year="{{ 'Pay &amp; start &mdash; $9,990/yr →' | t | safe }}">{% t %}Pay &amp; start &mdash; $999/mo →{% endt %}</button>
      </form>
      <input class="reserve-input buy-site" id="buySite" type="text" inputmode="url" autocomplete="url"
             placeholder="yourcompany.com" aria-label="{% t %}Your company website{% endt %}" hidden>
      <p class="reserve-msg is-error" id="buyMsg" hidden></p>
      <p class="buy-note">
        {#- #423 — the homepage tile's twin, and it changes with it. The
            reasoning is in src/index.njk beside the same line. -#}
        <span id="buyLive" hidden>{% t %}Next stop is Stripe. This address becomes your studio's sign-in — a work email brings your whole team in with you.{% endt %} </span>{% t %}Money back if it doesn't earn its keep — no fourteen days of hoping you remember to cancel.{% endt %}
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
{#- defer (#137). This sat mid-document without it, so the parser stopped
    here and rendered nothing below until the script had been fetched and
    run. Every one of these is an IIFE that looks its elements up by id and
    returns if they are absent, so deferring is not only cheaper — it is the
    case the guard was written for, with the DOM guaranteed complete. -#}
<script src="{{ '/assets/js/buy.js' | asset }}" defer></script>
{#- plan.js loads AFTER buy.js on purpose (#542): deferred scripts run in
    document order, so buy.js is listening for the switch's `plan` event by the
    time plan.js announces the choice a `?plan=` in the URL already made. -#}
<script src="{{ '/assets/js/plan.js' | asset }}" defer></script>

<section class="pricing-faq">
  <div class="pricing-faq-inner">
    <h2 class="sec-mid">{% t %}The money questions.{% endt %}</h2>
    <dl class="pricing-faq-list">
      <div class="pricing-faq-item">
        <dt>{% t %}What counts as a workspace?{% endt %}</dt>
        <dd>{% t %}One company you are selling <em>for</em> — its brief, its voice, its
          proof points, its pitches, its network. Selling for yourself means one
          workspace. An agency selling on behalf of five clients keeps five, so
          nobody's research, voice or network leaks into anybody else's.{% endt %}</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="pricing-faq-item">
        <dt>{% t %}How many seats do I get?{% endt %}</dt>
        <dd>{% t %}All of them. Pay with your work email and every colleague on that
          domain signs straight in — there is nothing to count and nothing to
          upgrade. Sign-in is Google or an emailed single-use link; there are no
          passwords.{% endt %}</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="pricing-faq-item">
        <dt>{% t %}Is there a run limit or a credit meter?{% endt %}</dt>
        <dd>{% t %}No. Runs are unlimited, and a batch takes up to a hundred companies
          at a time. We would rather you used it hard — a workspace that runs a
          lot and records what happened is a workspace that gets visibly better
          results, which is the point.{% endt %}</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="pricing-faq-item">
        {#- #423. The old answer said "no trial theater" and named the scan as
            the only free thing. Both had gone stale in different directions:
            #419 put a second free thing on the page (the run at /r), and #422
            built a free tier whose door opens on one operator env var. This
            answer is written to survive that flip — it denies the fourteen-day
            clock, which stays true, rather than denying that anything is free,
            which does not. -#}
        <dt>{% t %}Is there a free trial?{% endt %}</dt>
        <dd>{% t %}No clock, no. The scan on the homepage reads your own site and shows
          who it would hunt for you, and the
          <a href="https://studio.prospektor.ai/r">free run</a> names companies
          worth approaching — neither asks for an account or a card. The
          subscription you pay for up front and take the money back if it
          doesn't earn its keep, with no fourteen days of hoping you remember to
          cancel.{% endt %}</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="pricing-faq-item">
        <dt>{% t %}What does "money back if it doesn't earn its keep" mean?{% endt %}</dt>
        <dd>{% t %}Exactly what it says. Write to
          <a href="mailto:{{ site.email }}">{{ site.email }}</a> and we refund
          you. We would rather hear why than keep a month you regret.{% endt %}</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="pricing-faq-item">
        {#- #542. The one thing the yearly plan does NOT do yet: move a
            workspace that is already paying. Saying so on the page it is sold
            from is cheaper than a support thread that starts with a broken
            expectation, and it is the only honest answer until billing is
            self-serve. -#}
        <dt>{% t %}Can I switch between monthly and yearly?{% endt %}</dt>
        <dd>{% t %}Yes — email <a href="mailto:{{ site.email }}">{{ site.email }}</a> and
          we move you at your next renewal. There is no switch inside the studio
          yet, so today it is a reply from a human rather than a button.{% endt %}</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="pricing-faq-item">
        <dt>{% t %}What happens if I stop?{% endt %}</dt>
        <dd>{% t %}Nothing is held hostage. Export what you have, share the links you
          have shared, print what you need — and the workspace and everything in
          it is deleted when you ask. What we sell is next month's work, not
          custody of last month's.{% endt %}</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="pricing-faq-item">
        <dt>{% t %}What happens to my data?{% endt %}</dt>
        <dd>{% t %}Workspaces are strictly separated — your research, pitches and
          network are visible to your team only. Email addresses in a LinkedIn
          export are deleted at import, and your network never travels on a
          shared pitch. The <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> says the rest,
          plainly.{% endt %}</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cta">
  <h2 class="reveal">{% t %}Not sure yet? See what it'd <span class="accent">hunt</span> for you.{% endt %}</h2>
  <a href="{{ '/#scan' | localize }}" class="btn-cta reveal">{% t %}Scan your site — free{% endt %}</a>
</section>
